.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt DRM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm drm
.Nd the card and render nodes of the display and rendering devices
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In drm/drm.h
.In drm/drm_mode.h
.Pp
.Pa /dev/dri/card0
to
.Pa /dev/dri/card63
.Pp
.Pa /dev/dri/renderD128
to
.Pa /dev/dri/renderD191
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each display or rendering device a driver binds is a DRM device with
Linux 6.12's interface, which libdrm, Mesa and the compositors use: a card
node,
.Pa /dev/dri/cardN ,
major 226 and minor
.Ar N ,
and, when the driver renders, a render node,
.Pa /dev/dri/renderD Ns Ar M ,
minor
.Ar M
= 128 +
.Ar N .
A device takes the lowest free index when it registers, so its number
follows the order the drivers found their devices; a program finds a
device by what
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_VERSION
and
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GET_UNIQUE
say of it, never by its number.
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GET_UNIQUE
gives the bus name, such as
.Ql pci:0000:00:02.0 ,
only once the master has asked for interface 1.1 or later with
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SET_VERSION ,
and is empty before, which is how libdrm's
.Fn drmOpenByName
tells a card it may open.
.Pa /dev/dri
is listed only while it has a node.
.Pp
Each
.Xr open 2
of a node is a DRM file, which every descriptor of that open file
description shares: those of
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr fork 2
and a socket's
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS .
.Xr kcmp 2
with
.Dv KCMP_FILE
tells whether two descriptors are one DRM file.
The file holds its buffer handles, its master role and its events, which
go when its last descriptor closes.
An open with
.Dv O_EXCL
fails with
.Er EBUSY .
.Ss Ioctls
The calls are Linux's
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_*
numbers, type
.Ql d ,
with the core's numbers below
.Dv DRM_COMMAND_BASE
(0x40) and the driver's from it to 0xa0.
The argument is copied in and out by the direction both the call's number
and the kernel's row of it give, and a shorter argument than the kernel's
is extended with zeros, as Linux does for a program built against an
older header; the argument is copied out even when the call fails.
.Pp
Each row has Linux's flags, which are checked in this order:
.Dv DRM_ROOT_ONLY
calls need a privileged caller,
.Dv DRM_AUTH
calls on a card node an authenticated file,
.Dv DRM_MASTER
calls the master, and a call without
.Dv DRM_RENDER_ALLOW
is refused on a render node.
Each is
.Er EACCES
when it fails.
A render node needs no authentication and has no master: it is for
rendering into buffers, and the card node for the display.
.Pp
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_VERSION ,
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GET_UNIQUE ,
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GET_CLIENT ,
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GET_STATS ,
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GET_CAP ,
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SET_CLIENT_CAP
and
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SET_VERSION
answer as Linux's do.
A client capability of mode setting needs a driver with mode setting;
.Dv DRM_CLIENT_CAP_ATOMIC
is refused with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
to a program whose name begins with
.Ql X ,
as Linux refuses it to the X server's modesetting driver.
.Xr drm-kms 7
gives the mode-setting calls.
.Ss Master
The master of a card is the one DRM file that may set modes.
The first open of a card that has no master becomes master, with a master
record of its own.
The master gives up the role with
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_DROP_MASTER ,
and when its file closes.
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SET_MASTER
takes the role while no file holds it:
a file that was master, called by the process that opened it, or any file
of a privileged caller; other callers are refused with
.Er EACCES .
It is
.Er EBUSY
while another file is master, and 0 for the master itself.
A file that takes the role for the first time makes a master record of its
own; one that was master already takes its old record back.
While a file of the console's card holds the role, the console gives it
the screen and the keyboard, and takes them back when none does, as
.Xr console 4
describes under
.Sx DRM masters .
.Ss Authentication
A file of a card node is authenticated when it made its own master record
or its opener was privileged.
Another client gets a magic with
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GET_MAGIC ,
the lowest free number of its master record, from 1, passes it to the
master, and the master authenticates it with
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_AUTH_MAGIC .
A magic is spent by its first use, and a second
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_AUTH_MAGIC
with it is
.Er EINVAL ,
as is a magic of 0 or of another master record.
.Ss Buffers
A buffer object is a range of whole pages a file reaches by a handle, the
lowest free number from 1 in that file.
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GEM_CLOSE
drops a handle; the object goes when no handle, mapping or dma-buf holds
it.
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GEM_FLINK
gives the object a name, the lowest free on the device, which any
authenticated file of the device opens with
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GEM_OPEN
for a handle of its own; the name goes with the object's last handle.
.Pp
A dumb buffer is a buffer a program draws into with the processor, for
the display.
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATE_DUMB
takes
.Va width ,
.Va height
and
.Va bpp ,
each not 0, and gives the
.Va handle ,
the
.Va pitch ,
the bytes of a row,
.Va ( width
\(mu
.Va bpp
+ 7) / 8, and the
.Va size ,
.Va pitch
\(mu
.Va height
rounded up to the page.
A width, height and depth whose size does not fit in 32 bits is
.Er EINVAL ,
and a driver without dumb buffers answers
.Er ENOSYS ;
.Dv DRM_CAP_DUMB_BUFFER
says which.
The buffer's pages are zero.
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_MAP_DUMB
gives the offset at which
.Xr mmap 2
on the same node maps the buffer, and
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_DESTROY_DUMB
drops the handle, as
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GEM_CLOSE
does.
.Pp
A buffer's offset is a whole number of pages, the same for every file of
the device, from 4 GiB on x86-64 and from 16 MiB on i386.
A mapping must be shared and lie within the buffer, from its offset or
any page past it, and its file must hold a handle to the buffer; a mapping
holds the
buffer until it is unmapped, so destroying the handle leaves the mapping's
bytes in place.
The driver chooses the mapping's cache type: the dumb buffers of a display
are mapped write-combining, so stores go to memory in bursts, and a
program reads them back slowly.
.Ss PRIME
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_PRIME_HANDLE_TO_FD
exports a buffer handle as a dma-buf descriptor using a
.Vt struct drm_prime_handle :
.Va handle
selects the object,
.Va flags
accepts only
.Dv DRM_CLOEXEC
and
.Dv DRM_RDWR ,
and
.Va fd
receives the descriptor.
The first export chooses read-only access without
.Dv DRM_RDWR
and read/write access with it; further exports retain that access mode.
.Dv DRM_CLOEXEC
applies separately to each descriptor.
.Pp
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_PRIME_FD_TO_HANDLE
imports the dma-buf descriptor in
.Va fd
and returns its handle in
.Va handle ;
it ignores
.Va flags ,
as Linux does.
Both calls work on card and render nodes without authentication.
.Dv DRM_CAP_PRIME
reports
.Dv DRM_PRIME_CAP_IMPORT
and
.Dv DRM_PRIME_CAP_EXPORT .
.Pp
Repeated exports share one dma-buf, and repeated imports into the same DRM
file return the same handle while it remains open.
A self-import on the exporting device uses the original buffer object;
an import on another device shares its pages and implicit fences.
An imported buffer reexports the original dma-buf.
Sealed memfd ranges exported through
.Xr udmabuf 4
can be imported too.
A driver can refuse memory it cannot use.
.Pp
A dma-buf descriptor maps its pages with shared
.Xr mmap 2
at offsets within its size.
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_MAP_DUMB
refuses imported objects with
.Er EINVAL ;
map the dma-buf descriptor instead.
Handles, dma-buf descriptors and mappings hold their own references.
Closing the exporting file or all buffer handles leaves descriptors and
mappings valid; closing all descriptors leaves existing mappings valid
until their final unmap.
.Ss Events
.Xr read 2
gives the file's queued events, each a
.Vt struct drm_event
and its body, as many whole events as fit; when the first does not fit it
returns 0.
It blocks until one is queued, unless the descriptor is
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
which answers
.Er EAGAIN .
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLIN
while an event is queued.
A file holds at most 4096 bytes of events and of events reserved for it.
.Ss Unplug
When a device goes, its nodes leave
.Pa /dev/dri
and its open files stay with it: their ioctls and reads answer
.Er ENODEV ,
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLERR
and
.Dv POLLHUP ,
and a touch of a mapping of its buffers raises
.Dv SIGBUS .
The device is freed when its last file and its last mapping go.
.Sh FILES
.Pa /dev/dri/cardN
has mode 0660 and
.Pa /dev/dri/renderDM
mode 0666 when the driver registers them, owned by root and its group.
On the GNU system udev then gives the card the group video and the render
node the group render with mode 0660, as Debian's rules give them on
Linux.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EOPNOTSUPP
.It Er EACCES
The file lacks what the call's flags need: privilege, authentication, the
master role or a card node; or
.Xr mmap 2
of a buffer the file holds no handle to.
.It Er EBADF
PRIME import of a descriptor that is not open.
.It Er EBUSY
An open with
.Dv O_EXCL ,
or
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SET_MASTER
while another file is master.
.It Er EFAULT
The argument cannot be read or written.
.It Er EINVAL
Unknown PRIME export flags, PRIME import of another descriptor type or
unusable memory, MAP_DUMB of an imported object,
a call the core and the driver do not have, an argument out of range, a
magic unknown or spent,
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GEM_CLOSE
of a handle the file does not hold, or
.Xr mmap 2
at an offset in no buffer, past its buffer's end, or private.
.It Er ENODEV
The device was unplugged, or
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_MAP_DUMB
of a buffer nothing can map, such as the firmware's framebuffer that
.Xr i915 4
adopts.
.It Er ENOENT
A handle the file does not hold, for
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GEM_FLINK ,
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_PRIME_HANDLE_TO_FD
and
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_MAP_DUMB ,
or a name no buffer has, for
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GEM_OPEN .
.It Er ENOSYS
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATE_DUMB
on a driver without dumb buffers.
.It Er ENOTTY
A call of another type than
.Ql d .
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
A feature the driver does not have, such as buffers or mode setting.
.El
